#!/bin/sh
# script/board-console: read a real board's serial console, log every byte, and stop on a
# deadline (milestone 216).
#
#   script/board-console                      # watch until the kernel banner, 120s cap
#   script/board-console --for 30m --until none   # sustained watching, for a stress run
#   script/board-console --replay target/board-console-1756...log   # re-read a capture
#   script/board-console --tally target/radon-soak-1757...log        # what the lottery drew (249)
#
# `script/console` is QEMU. This is the other one: an actual serial port on an actual board, at
# 115200 8N1, with the log written to a file whatever happens. It recognises the boot sequence
# notes/visionfive2.md's bench runbook records (SPL, OpenSBI, U-Boot, `Starting kernel ...`, ours)
# and returns a different exit status for each way a session can end, so a bench script can tell
# them apart:
#
#   0  reached the stage asked for (or watched the whole duration with nothing announced)
#   1  the board announced a failure: a bad image header, a measured-boot refusal, a kernel panic
#   2  it spoke and then went quiet, which is what a hang looks like from this end
#   3  the time ran out with the stage unreached
#   4  the port could not be opened, or the arguments were wrong
#
# It reads and never writes to the board, and it does not touch power. That is still true with
# `--tally`, which touches no port at all: it reads a capture that has already finished and reports
# what the thread-placement lottery drew on each boot in it (milestone 249). A tally has two exit
# statuses, 0 and 4, because there is no board left to have gone quiet.
#
# See notes/board-console.md for the runbook and for what the recognition can and cannot tell
# apart, and notes/soak.md for the rebooting soak the tally is written for.
#
# Name: provisional, this lane's coinage (2026-09-01, milestone 216). Hyphens because script/
# entry points are hyphenated (AGENTS.md's naming table), and the `board-` prefix to sit beside
# `board-image`, the other entry point here that is about a real board; the pair now reads as one
# family, build the payload and then watch it boot. `console` alone was already taken by the QEMU
# one and could not be reused. `serial` was refused as a word for the wire rather than for the
# thing. Expect the integrator to surface it.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

exec cargo xtask board-console "$@"
